<div style="font-size:11px;color:#56585B;padding:7px 4px;">HDQ calculation using four equal biweekly payments per new order. Includes a separately scheduled $120 debit. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>The Retail Council of Canada and Leger found that 88% of respondents ranked Black Friday among their top three shopping days. The survey measured intentions. It did not track how people actually financed orders or whether borrowers missed instalments. The timing question matters even if every payment goes through and the advertised financing really costs zero.</p>
<h2>Two orders, one crowded pay period</h2>
<p>Consider a $480 purchase on Nov. 27 and a $320 purchase on Dec. 4, each with four equal interest-free payments. Assume that the first payment is taken at checkout and the rest every 14 days, an available structure exemplified by Affirm Canada’s Shop Pay guidance. The first order takes $120 on Nov. 27, Dec. 11, Dec. 25 and Jan. 8. The second takes $80 on Dec. 4, Dec. 18, Jan. 1 and Jan. 15. On that staggered schedule the charges do not land on the same date. That is precisely what makes funding dates a better test than a list of due dates.</p>
<p>Suppose a biweekly paycheck arrives Nov. 20, Dec. 4, Dec. 18, Jan. 1 and Jan. 15, with funds available from the start of each pay period. Each of those three periods carries $200. In that case, even though the purchases were staggered, the total is predictable and there is no unusual peak. The meaningful test is whether a second household commitment also lands within one of those windows.</p>
<p>HDQ added a third, already scheduled $120 instalment from an earlier purchase on Dec. 15, while holding the two Black Friday offers fixed. In the Dec. 4–17 pay period, the Dec. 4 and Dec. 11 amounts sum to $200 and the existing Dec. 15 payment lifts the cash required to $320. That is $80 more than a $240 reserve. The existing purchase is an additional obligation. It does not increase the price of the two new orders. Across the four modelled pay periods starting Nov. 20, Dec. 4, Dec. 18 and Jan. 1, total instalment outflows are $120, $320, $200 and $200. The final $80 is due Jan. 15, at the beginning of the next pay period.</p>
<h2>Put funding dates above repayment dates</h2>
<p>The Financial Consumer Agency of Canada says terms vary and fees or higher interest can apply under some agreements. Its guidance makes it important to verify the first debit, frequency, any conditions attached to the promotional rate and how a return affects future payments. The example uses scheduled dates, which may be adjusted by a provider or payment network. It does not assume that a holiday cancels a payment.</p>
<p>An instalment plan can improve liquidity if the money is already earmarked or arrives before each debit. It can also make a purchase appear easier to fund if the shopper compares the instalment only with today’s bank balance. A useful client conversation starts with existing automatic payments, maps both new schedules into the same pay windows and checks the largest combined draw after essential bills.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
<ul><li><a href="https://www.retailcouncil.org/press-release/canadians-plan-with-purpose-put-loved-ones-first-and-shop-smart-for-value/">Retail Council of Canada and Leger, 2026 Holiday Shopping Survey, October 2026</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/loans/buy-now-pay-later.html">Financial Consumer Agency of Canada, Buy now, pay later plans, October 14, 2025</a></li>
<li><a href="https://helpcenter.affirm.ca/s/article/about-shop-pay-installments-sp-ca?language=en_US">Affirm Canada, About Shop Pay Installments</a></li></ul>',
