<div style="font-size:11px;color:#56585B;padding:7px 4px;">HDQ scenarios using CRA rules. Assumes eligibility, no prior FHSA and no other room adjustments. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>HDQ compared openings in December 2026 and January 2027 under the standard rules. Open in December and use no room, and 2027 starts with $8,000 of new room and up to $8,000 carried forward. Open in January, and 2027 starts with $8,000. The December opening creates capacity to put an extra $8,000 in the account in 2027. It creates no deduction for 2026 without a qualifying contribution.</p>
<h2>How much does the head start matter?</h2>
<p>Take a buyer able to set aside $12,000 in 2027. With an unused December opening, the entire $12,000 generally fits, leaving $4,000 of unused room. A January opening has room for $8,000, leaving $4,000 outside the FHSA for the time being. If next year’s savings amount to $8,000 or less, both opening dates can accommodate that money in 2027. If they exceed $16,000, even the early opening will leave some money outside the account.</p>
<p>A different scenario isolates the effect of funding this year. Someone who opens and contributes $3,000 in December generally carries $5,000 into 2027 and has $13,000 of room that year. A person who uses all $8,000 in December generally begins 2027 with $8,000. Both can put a combined $16,000 into the account across the two calendar years if eligible and otherwise within the limits. Where the contribution lands matters for cash flow and for the first tax year in which a deduction can be available. The usual lifetime ceiling stays $40,000.</p>
<h2>The calendar also runs in the other direction</h2>
<p>The maximum participation period ends at the end of the calendar year containing the earliest applicable event. CRA lists the 15th anniversary of the first opening, turning 71 and the year after the first qualifying withdrawal. With the anniversary as the binding condition, the two opening dates move the applicable December deadline from 2042 to 2041. Opening earlier has a cost for someone whose prospective purchase is much further out. If the home purchase never happens, a properly executed direct transfer to the holder’s RRSP or RRIF generally defers tax subject to the applicable conditions. A withdrawal paid to the holder can have different consequences.</p>
<p>Advisors can compare realistic 2027 savings with the $8,000 threshold, then confirm eligibility, the institution’s actual opening date and any activity affecting room. Anyone who already has an FHSA needs a different calculation based on their existing records. An eligible new opener should also record the opening for Schedule 15 even if the December funding line is zero.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
<ul><li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/individuals/topics/first-home-savings-account/opening-your-fhsas.html">Canada Revenue Agency, Opening your FHSAs</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/individuals/topics/first-home-savings-account/contributing-your-fhsa.html">Canada Revenue Agency, Participating in your FHSAs</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/individuals/topics/first-home-savings-account/closing-your-fhsa.html">Canada Revenue Agency, Closing your FHSA</a></li>
