</script>
<div style="font-size:11px;color:#56585B;padding:7px 4px;">HDQ calculation from Statistics Canada Table 1, Nov. 25 vintage. Seasonally adjusted current dollars. Rest of Canada is inferred from the national and Quebec totals. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>HDQ subtracted August receipts from September receipts using Statistics Canada’s published table. The resulting national gain was $14.460 million. Quebec fell by $12.183 million, implying a $26.643 million gain for the rest of Canada when derived as the national change less Quebec’s. On that basis, Quebec offset 45.7% of the net growth elsewhere. Independently adjusted or rounded rows can differ slightly from the national total, so the rest-of-Canada number here uses the published national and Quebec rows consistently.</p>
<h2>Size changes the ranking</h2>
<p>Newfoundland and Labrador recorded the largest percentage increase among the provinces, 2.2%, but it added $2.182 million. British Columbia’s 0.4% gain represented $6.471 million. Ontario added $4.183 million and Alberta $3.989 million. Together those three provinces added $14.643 million, just above the net Canadian increase after gains and losses elsewhere. Percentage changes are useful for judging the relative speed of a local move. Dollar changes explain how much that move matters to the national result.</p>
<p>Quebec was down for the month, yet it was up 5.6% compared with September 2025, close to Canada’s 5.8% year-over-year growth. Its September level was also above June in the same release. The monthly divergence is real in the published estimates. Those comparison windows keep it from becoming a claim that Quebec’s restaurant market was in a sustained contraction.</p>
<h2>The format of the business matters too</h2>
<p>Nationally, full-service restaurants added $14.433 million and limited-service outlets added $8.269 million. Special food services fell $8.259 million while drinking places were nearly flat. The full-service gain happened to be almost identical to the total Canadian gain after the other formats offset one another. The release presents these geographic and format breakdowns separately, and the full November 25 table has only the aggregate for Quebec’s seasonally adjusted receipts in both months. It does not establish which format caused the Quebec decline.</p>
<p>The numbers track seasonally adjusted receipts in current dollars, and the September figures are preliminary. Restaurant meal prices were up 3.1% year over year in the unadjusted CPI. Applying that annual price change to a 0.2% monthly sales figure would mix different time periods and baskets. Even the annual numbers do not reveal how many people came through the door, how much they ordered, or whether margins improved.</p>
<p>For an advisor working with an operator, the place to start is the operator’s own weekly sales bridge. Transactions, average bill, promotions and the split between dine-in and other channels can help identify whether a weaker month changed working-capital needs. For a multi-location business, do the work location by location before aggregating. September’s release shows why the consolidated headline can hide moves of greater practical consequence.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
<ul><li><a href="https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/261125/dq261125b-eng.htm">Statistics Canada, Food services and drinking places, September 2026, released November 25, 2026</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/261125/t001b-eng.htm">Statistics Canada, Table 1, Food services and drinking places, seasonally adjusted, November 25 release vintage</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/t1/tbl1/en/tv.action?pid=2110001901">Statistics Canada, Monthly survey of food services and drinking places, table 21-10-0019-01</a></li></ul>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Owners whose own receipts are weak can feel at odds with an upbeat national release, especially when the local results point the other way.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The national figure is a sum of different markets. Let’s reconcile your transactions, average bill and receipts location by location.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p>Quebec restaurant operators</p>
<p>Owners with multiple locations or service formats</p>
<p>Businesses preparing a cash-flow or credit review</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Compare weekly receipts against a matching local period.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Separate transaction counts from average bill and promotions.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review cash balances and scheduled outflows by location.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Allow for revisions when citing Statistics Canada’s preliminary September figure.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Compare your receipts with local conditions<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
September’s national gain included a Quebec decline, and the release cannot identify your guest traffic. We can review your own counts and receipts together to see what changed.<br><br>
Would you have 15 minutes to talk this week?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p>Quebec restaurant operators</p>
<p>Owners with multiple locations or service formats</p>
<p>Businesses preparing a cash-flow or credit review</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Did your September receipts move because of transaction counts, average spend or location mix?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A local sales bridge helps turn a headline into a working-capital discussion grounded in the operator’s own data.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
