INSERT OR REPLACE INTO articles
  (slug, desk, article_type, title, dek, brief_html, body_html, respond_html, prospect_html, key_numbers, hero_image, hero_caption, read_time, published_at, tags, toolkit_gated, sources_text)
VALUES (
  '2026/11/26/us-section-301-eu-tariff-top-up',
  'geo',
  'article',
  'The Proposed 15% U.S. Tariff Could Add Five Points to Some EU Goods',
  'Canada is absent from the new U.S. proposal. Canadian firms with foreign-origin goods in their U.S. supply chains still have an exposure to check at the product level.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>USTR issued determinations for 14 economies.</strong><span> Canada was outside the original 16 investigations and Mexico received no determination in this announcement.</span></li>
<li><strong>Fifteen percent describes a combined rate for certain goods.</strong><span> The EU proposal nets out ordinary MFN rates and existing Section 301 rates.</span></li>
<li><strong>The remaining increase can be smaller.</strong><span> If the two counted duty layers are 5% each, the incremental charge would be five percentage points at the stated rate.</span></li>
<li><strong>Exemptions can change the answer.</strong><span> Covered Section 232 goods or products listed in a relevant annex need separate treatment.</span></li>
<li><strong>The process is still open.</strong><span> Written comments are due Jan. 5 and hearings begin Jan. 25, 2027.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The U.S. trade representative on Wednesday proposed a new set of tariffs after finding that practices in 14 economies created or sustained excess industrial capacity. Canada was outside the original investigations. Mexico was investigated and received no determination in Wednesday’s announcement. That makes the first Canadian question concrete. Does a business have goods of an affected origin entering the United States somewhere in its supply chain?</p>
<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #D0D0D0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
<div style="background:#F9F9F9;border-bottom:1px solid #D0D0D0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
<span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#4A5568;letter-spacing:0.02em;">What would be added at a 15% combined rate?</span>
<span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#4A5568;">5 pts</span>
<span style="font-size:13px;color:#4A5568;">With 10 points already counted</span>
<span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">PERCENTAGE POINTS | EU SCENARIOS</span>
</div>
<div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;">
<script>
(function(){
  var mount=document.currentScript;
  var ns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'';
  var svg=document.createElementNS(ns,''svg'');
  svg.setAttribute(''viewBox'',''0 0 680 300'');
  svg.setAttribute(''width'',''100%'');
  svg.setAttribute(''role'',''img'');
  svg.setAttribute(''aria-label'',"What would be added at a 15% combined rate?");
  svg.setAttribute(''font-family'',''system-ui, sans-serif'');
  [["rect",{"x":0,"y":0,"width":680,"height":300,"fill":"#FFFFFF","rx":0},null],["line",{"x1":66,"y1":232,"x2":623,"y2":232,"stroke":"#D0D0D0","stroke-width":1,"stroke-dasharray":""},null],["text",{"x":55,"y":236,"fill":"#4A5568","text-anchor":"end","font-size":13,"font-weight":400},"0"],["line",{"x1":66,"y1":172,"x2":623,"y2":172,"stroke":"#D0D0D0","stroke-width":1,"stroke-dasharray":""},null],["text",{"x":55,"y":176,"fill":"#4A5568","text-anchor":"end","font-size":13,"font-weight":400},"5"],["line",{"x1":66,"y1":112,"x2":623,"y2":112,"stroke":"#D0D0D0","stroke-width":1,"stroke-dasharray":""},null],["text",{"x":55,"y":116,"fill":"#4A5568","text-anchor":"end","font-size":13,"font-weight":400},"10"],["line",{"x1":66,"y1":52,"x2":623,"y2":52,"stroke":"#D0D0D0","stroke-width":1,"stroke-dasharray":""},null],["text",{"x":55,"y":56,"fill":"#4A5568","text-anchor":"end","font-size":13,"font-weight":400},"15"],["path",{"d":"M 116 52 L 269 112 L 422 172 L 575 232","fill":"none","stroke":"#1A3560","stroke-width":1.8},null],["circle",{"cx":116,"cy":52,"r":4.5,"fill":"#1A3560"},null],["text",{"x":116,"y":257,"fill":"#4A5568","text-anchor":"middle","font-size":13,"font-weight":400},"0"],["text",{"x":116,"y":42,"fill":"#4A5568","text-anchor":"middle","font-size":13,"font-weight":600},"15"],["circle",{"cx":269,"cy":112,"r":4.5,"fill":"#1A3560"},null],["text",{"x":269,"y":257,"fill":"#4A5568","text-anchor":"middle","font-size":13,"font-weight":400},"5"],["text",{"x":269,"y":102,"fill":"#4A5568","text-anchor":"middle","font-size":13,"font-weight":600},"10"],["circle",{"cx":422,"cy":172,"r":4.5,"fill":"#1A3560"},null],["text",{"x":422,"y":257,"fill":"#4A5568","text-anchor":"middle","font-size":13,"font-weight":400},"10"],["circle",{"cx":575,"cy":232,"r":4.5,"fill":"#1A3560"},null],["text",{"x":575,"y":257,"fill":"#4A5568","text-anchor":"middle","font-size":13,"font-weight":400},"15"],["text",{"x":575,"y":222,"fill":"#4A5568","text-anchor":"middle","font-size":13,"font-weight":600},"0"],["rect",{"x":433,"y":152,"width":51,"height":16,"fill":"#E8A825","rx":7},null],["text",{"x":458.5,"y":164,"fill":"#242628","text-anchor":"middle","font-size":11,"font-weight":700},"5 pts"],["text",{"x":343,"y":281,"fill":"#4A5568","text-anchor":"middle","font-size":13,"font-weight":400},"Points in duties already counted under the proposal"]].forEach(function(row){
    var node=document.createElementNS(ns,row[0]);
    Object.keys(row[1]).forEach(function(key){node.setAttribute(key,row[1][key]);});
    if(row[2]!==null){node.textContent=row[2];}
    svg.appendChild(node);
  });
  mount.parentNode.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
<div style="font-size:11px;color:#56585B;padding:7px 4px;">HDQ calculations from the Nov. 25 USTR proposal. Illustrative non-exempt EU-origin goods under the stated scenario. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>The second question is how much of the headline rate would actually be new. USTR’s proposed 15% rate for the European Union, Japan, South Korea, Switzerland and Taiwan is net of applicable most-favoured-nation rates and existing Section 301 duties. The proposal treats some other economies differently. For Bangladesh, for example, the proposed 18% rate nets out existing Section 301 duties while leaving ordinary customs duties outside that calculation. A common corporate assumption about the meaning of a quoted rate could send the same finance team in different directions across two suppliers.</p>
<h2>Applying the rate to an entry</h2>
<p>HDQ calculated an illustrative case for a non-exempt, EU-origin product entered at US$100,000 in customs value. Assume the applicable MFN rate is 5%, the existing Section 301 duty is 5% and the proposal takes effect as written at the stated rate. Existing counted duties would be US$10,000. The incremental duty would be US$5,000, taking the counted duties to US$15,000. Treating the announcement as an additional 15% would put US$15,000 of new cost into the scenario and overstate the increase by US$10,000.</p>
<p>Vary the pre-existing counted layers and the result changes. At a combined 0%, 5%, 10% and 15%, the illustrative top-ups are 15, 10, five and zero percentage points. These cases are arithmetic, not classifications for individual entries. Other charges, including applicable trade-remedy duties, can have their own rules. The chart shows how the offset works and makes no claim about the prevalence of any starting rate.</p>
<h2>The Canadian connection runs through the contract</h2>
<p>A head office in Canada does not identify the origin of an imported product. A U.S. affiliate may import a finished good from an affected economy, or a Canadian exporter’s U.S. buyer may import an affected input in a separate transaction. A shipment involving production or handling in Canada requires the applicable origin analysis, including the nature of any processing. An address or shipping route alone cannot settle it.</p>
<p>Wiley’s trade-law analysis also identifies proposed exclusions for certain goods already subject to Section 232 and for goods listed in economy-specific annexes. An exclusion from this proposal can leave existing duties in place. Because the rates and exemptions can change through consultation or negotiation, contingency estimates should be tied to the relevant draft and separated from amounts already payable.</p>
<p>A Canadian advisor working with an importer can ask for one actual entry summary and its corresponding purchase contract. Record who imports the goods, the declared customs value, tariff classification, origin, current duties and who bears an increase. Then repeat for the largest entries. That gives a finance team a way to assess exposure before the Jan. 5 comment deadline or the Jan. 25 hearing, without assuming any duty proposed Wednesday is already being collected.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
<ul><li><a href="https://ustr.gov/about/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2026/november/ustr-makes-determinations-and-proposes-actions-14-section-301-investigations-relating-structural">Office of the U.S. Trade Representative, Section 301 excess-capacity determinations and proposed actions, November 25, 2026</a></li>
