<div style="font-size:11px;color:#56585B;padding:7px 4px;">TMX and NYSE holiday calendars and standard hours. Regular and core sessions only. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>NYSE and Nasdaq are closed for the U.S. holiday. On Friday, their regular stock sessions end at 1 p.m. Eastern. The TSX calendar shows the holiday as a U.S. holiday with special settlement for affected U.S.-dollar issues. Toronto’s regular 9:30 a.m. to 4 p.m. equity session leaves zero shared regular hours on Thursday and 3½ hours on Friday. HDQ calculated that Friday has three Canadian trading hours after the U.S. core close. The figures compare regular sessions and exclude extended-hours sessions and overnight futures.</p>
<h2>The execution question</h2>
<p>Vanguard Canada and RBC Global Asset Management both describe the pricing problem when a Canadian ETF trades while its underlying market is closed. A market maker has less direct price information and may quote a wider spread. The result can differ by fund, holdings, order size and the clarity of other price signals. It does not follow that every U.S. equity ETF will quote a large spread today.</p>
<p>A screen can also show an apparent divergence from the underlying stocks’ last prices, particularly when currency or other information changes after those prices were recorded. Compare like timestamps and distinguish the bid and ask from the last trade. A limit order can control the worst permitted execution price, though the order may then go unfilled. For perspective, a 20-cent displayed spread on a $100 ETF is 20 basis points wide. An order at the ask would be 10 basis points above the displayed midpoint, before any market impact or price movement. That is an illustration of the quote, not a forecast of today’s spread or an estimate of fair value.</p>
<h2>The operations question</h2>
<p>TMX separately designated Friday, Nov. 27, as the settlement date for Wednesday’s affected U.S.-dollar securities trades. On a standard next-business-day assumption, Thursday might have been the expected date. The notice says the corresponding Canadian-dollar line, where available, is unaffected by those special rules. A Canadian-dollar ETF that owns U.S. stocks can still face the pricing issue, because trading currency and the underlying market’s hours describe different things.</p>
<p>For an advisor, the practical distinction is to check liquidity at the point of execution and cash availability at the broker before committing the proceeds elsewhere. A routine allocation rebalance has more scope to wait for shared market hours than an urgent liquidity need. If a trade is time-sensitive, verify the live quote, the currency of the listed line and the confirmed settlement date. Holiday hours alone cannot answer all three.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
